Simulated medium profiles
-------------------------

`--debug --sim-cd=image.iso` retains its original CD-R behavior. Add
`--sim-profile=0x43` for BD-RE or `--sim-profile=0x1a` for DVD+RW.
The option accepts decimal or hexadecimal MMC profile numbers:

  0x08 CD-ROM   0x09 CD-R    0x0a CD-RW
  0x10 DVD-ROM  0x1a DVD+RW  0x1b DVD+R
  0x40 BD-ROM   0x41 BD-R   0x43 BD-RE

Other profiles are rejected. DVD and BD simulations supply minimal single-layer
disc structures and use READ(10), including the same missing-sector and error
simulation as READ CD. The image length determines the reported capacity;
append sectors to model unused formatted space. This is a read-only test
device, not a simulation of formatting, writing, or every MMC command.
`DVDISASTER_SCSI_SIMULATED_NODELAY=1` disables the synthetic read delay for
all profiles (the regression runner sets it automatically).

The RS03i profile tests cover padded images on BD-RE/DVD+RW, the non-rewritable
and non-exhaustive search gates, stale ECC, unreadable fingerprints, both reader
entry points, and missing-ECC hints with and without matching external ECC.


Encoder backend tests
---------------------

After configuring, run `make check-encoders`. This checks every available
encoder, including automatic selection, against a separate polynomial-form
shift register using GF(0x187) multiplication. Coverage includes all shift
positions at representative root counts, all feedback bytes, zero feedback,
unaligned buffers for SIMD backends, buffer guards, tails and complete
codewords. Padding bytes are scratch space and are not compared.

On AArch64, configure automatically enables the NEON encoder. Use
--with-neon=no to disable it or --with-neon=yes to require compiler support.
The probe compiles and links without executing ARM code or adding ISA flags.
This option controls encoding only; the existing AArch64 syndrome kernel
is independent. Select the encoder with --encoding-algorithm NEON or auto.
The test executable also accepts one backend name, e.g. `test-encoders NEON`,
so cross-built tests can be run explicitly through qemu-aarch64.


Syndrome backend tests
----------------------

After configuring, run `make check-syndromes` from the repository root.
This compares complete syndromes against independent multiplication using
polynomial 0x187, including unaligned buffers and nonzero offsets. On GFNI
hosts it also checks every byte value for all 255 nonzero multipliers using
the matrices created by the production code.

The test restricts runtime CPU features to exercise the fallback, AVX2 and
GFNI paths separately. Backends unavailable in the build or on the host
are skipped; the fallback is scalar on x86 and NEON on AArch64.
Configure with --with-gfni=no to omit GFNI, or --with-gfni=yes to require
compiler support. Default detection compiles and links without executing
GFNI instructions. This setting is independent of --with-avx2: the GFNI
kernel still requires AVX2 and GFNI CPU support at runtime.


CRC32 backend tests
-------------------

On POSIX systems, run `make check-crc32` after configuring. The test compares
production dispatch, the software implementation, and any available ARM kernel
against an independent bitwise IEEE CRC32 reference. Coverage includes the
uncomplemented known vector, empty input, random lengths and alignments, buffers
ending at an inaccessible page, and eight concurrent first callers. A second
test binary masks the ARM capability to exercise runtime software fallback.

Linux AArch64 builds automatically enable hardware CRC32 when the compiler and
headers support it. `--with-arm-crc32=no` omits the backend; `--with-arm-crc32=yes`
requires compiler support. Configure compiles the exact kernel without executing
it or enabling optional instructions globally. Runtime selection uses HWCAP_CRC32.
Other platforms retain slicing-by-8. The format's IEEE polynomial, initial value,
absence of final complement, and little-endian storage remain unchanged.

Use `build/test-crc32 --require-arm` to require hardware execution on a suitable
host. `gcc -O2 -DCRC32_ARM_STANDALONE regtest/crc32.c -pthread -o /tmp/test-crc32-arm`
also tests the kernel without GLib, but does not cover application dispatch.
The test prints a small software-versus-hardware timing comparison; use complete
application workloads to evaluate user-visible gains.


General structure of the database log file
------------------------------------------

Creating a new database file:
Run the test, then copy and modify /dev/shm/newlog.txt

- The first 2 lines of the log file contain special content
  and are excluded from the diff against the actual log.

Line 1: md5sum of image file or "ignore"
Line 2: md5sum of ecc file or "ignore"

- Remove directory path prefix of all occurrences of .iso and .ecc files!


Anatomy of a test case
----------------------

Example:

01 # Scan an image with a simulated hardware failure and 
02 # --ignore-fatal-sense not set.
03
04 if try "scanning image, ignoring simulated hardware failure" scan_with_ignored_hardware_failure; then
05
06   cp $MASTERISO $SIMISO
07   $NEWVER --debug -i$SIMISO --erase "5000:hardware failure" >>$LOGFILE 2>&1
08
09   replace_config ignore-fatal-sense 1
10   extra_args="--debug --sim-cd=$SIMISO --fixed-speed-values"
11   run_regtest scan_with_ignored_hardware_failure "--spinup-delay=0 -s --ignore-fatal-sense" $ISODIR/no.iso  $ISODIR/no.ecc
12 fi

line 01-02: Describe what the test case does and what results are expected.
line 04   : try description symbol
            provides a descriptive text for the test case and defines the symbol/label
            which is part of the database file and the config.txt list.
line 06-07: prepare test files
line 09   : in GUI mode, some settings can only made in the .dvdisaster file,
     	    not with the respective command line switches
line 10   : arguments which work both in GUI mode and command line mode
line 11   : run_regtest symbol args iso_image ecc_file
     	    Runs the regtest.
	    In GUI mode, args is ignored and only the iso_image and ecc_file parameters
	    are passed via the configuration file. extra_args are provided on the
	    command line.
	    In CLI mode, args is prepended with extra_args and appended with switches
	    for the iso_image and the ecc_file and then passed with the program
	    call in the command line. 	    An optional fifth argument may contain the words SORTED (sort the log
	    before comparing it) and EXITCODE (append the program's exit status
	    to the log; CLI mode only).
